hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.15) stable; urgency=medium

  * librespot: carry two Spotify Connect volume fixes on top of v0.8.0,
    applied from librespot-patches/ at build time until they are released
    upstream. Both patches were written and contributed by alepee.
  * librespot: report the volume to the other devices at most 500 ms after a
    change. The delayed update was a debounce, pushed back by every incoming
    command, so while a client scrolled its volume slider the device
    reported nothing until the scrolling stopped. The Spotify app then
    reverted its slider to the last reported volume a second or two later,
    and the next scroll step started from there: the volume moved less than
    asked and the slider jumped back. Dragging the slider sent fewer
    commands, which is why it worked better.
  * librespot: start at the ALSA mixer's current volume when no
    --initial-volume is given, as its help already says. It fell back to
    50%, and since Spirc sets the mixer to its initial volume, every start
    and every reconnect reset the hardware volume to 50% whatever another
    source (Roon, the HiFiBerry UI) had set it to, and the Spotify app showed
    a level that did not match what the speakers played.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 07:12:06 +0000

hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.14) stable; urgency=medium

  * start-librespot: do not pass the audiocontrol access token when the
    account behind it is not premium. librespot refuses a free account and
    exits 1, and the unit restarted it every ten seconds without end: with
    RestartSec=10 and StartLimitBurst=5 in 30 s at most three starts fit the
    window, so the start limit never trips, and each start logged in to
    Spotify again. The wrapper now asks Spotify's /v1/me for the account's
    product first and, for "free" or "open", starts without the token, on
    zeroconf discovery and the cache, so a premium account selecting the
    player from a Spotify client still works. A lookup that fails or returns
    no product says nothing about the account, and the token is used as
    before.
  * start-librespot: set aside cached credentials that librespot logs in
    with and then refuses as a free account. Since 0.8.0.13 the credentials
    of the token's account are saved by --system-cache, so dropping the token
    alone would have looped on the cache instead. The refusal is not a
    "Login failed" line, which the filter watched for, but
    'librespot does not support "free" accounts'; it is now matched too.
    (start-librespot.sh v1.1.4). Covered by tests/start-librespot.sh.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.13) stable; urgency=medium

  * start-librespot: pass --system-cache, so librespot persists the
    credentials it receives over zeroconf. Without it they only ever live in
    memory: a restarted player has nothing to authenticate with and stays
    silent until a Spotify client selects it again and pushes them back. The
    only other non-zeroconf path is the audiocontrol token the wrapper reads
    once before exec and passes as --access-token, and that one Spotify
    expires after an hour and librespot cannot refresh from here, since the
    refresh token belongs to audiocontrol. This is not the audio cache:
    --disable-audio-cache stays on, and --system-cache stores only
    credentials.json and volume, a few hundred bytes in total.
    (start-librespot.sh v1.1.3; alepee)
  * start-librespot: the directory follows the split start-raat and
    start-squeezelite already use -- ~/.librespot under a user service,
    /var/lib/librespot otherwise, which is also where spotify-event.sh
    looks. SYSTEM_CACHE overrides the path, or disables persistence when set
    to an empty value. Its mode is set to 0700 on every start, not only when
    the directory is created, since librespot writes credentials.json
    without a mode of its own and the directory is the only thing keeping it
    off other local accounts. On a device upgraded from 0.7.1.1 or earlier
    /var/lib/librespot already exists, created 0775 librespot:audio by the
    postinst of the day for the event pipe, so a mode applied only at
    creation would never reach it. A directory that cannot be created or
    secured warns and persistence is skipped: a player that starts without
    persistence is better than one that does not start, and credentials do
    not belong in a directory whose mode could not be set.
  * start-librespot: set cached credentials aside when Spotify explicitly
    rejects them. librespot logs in with the cache at startup and, when the
    account refuses it, exits 1 without touching the file, so every restart
    read the same credentials and failed again ten seconds later, before
    discovery could ever receive replacements. The wrapper now runs
    librespot's stderr through a filter that forwards every line unchanged
    and renames credentials.json to credentials.json.rejected on "Bad
    credentials", "Could not validate credentials" or "Premium account
    required", so the next start falls back to zeroconf discovery. Network
    and audio failures, running out of access points, and any other nonzero
    exit keep the credentials. The filter is only armed when the player logs
    in from the cache: an audiocontrol token takes precedence, and its
    rejection says nothing about the cached file. Covered by
    tests/start-librespot.sh, run in a disposable debian:trixie container.
  * start-librespot: as a side effect librespot now also restores its cached
    volume at startup, but only when it falls back to softvol. With the ALSA
    mixer, which is the nominal path here, the cached volume is ignored and
    the hardware volume still wins.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:32:24 +0000

hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.12) stable; urgency=medium

  * postinst: stop writing the WebUI descriptor and icon into
    /etc/hifiberry/players.d. 0.8.0.10 moved both to
    /usr/share/hifiberry/players.d because a descriptor is package data, but
    the postinst kept writing the /etc copy on every configure, and
    configurator lets /etc shadow /usr/share -- so the packaged descriptor was
    masked on every device and no device ever read it. The two had drifted:
    the shipped one declares conflicts_with ["soloist"], the /etc copy did
    not, so that conflict was silently inert wherever this package was
    installed. It also clobbered any deliberate administrator override on
    every upgrade, which is the one thing /etc is meant to allow.
  * postinst: remove the stale /etc copy when upgrading from an affected
    version, since nothing else will -- it stopped being a conffile in
    0.8.0.10, so dpkg does not track it. A file present on a fresh install is
    left alone: there it is an administrator's own override.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 09:22:54 +0000

