hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.10) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the WebUI player descriptor and icon from
    /usr/share/hifiberry/players.d instead of /etc. A descriptor is package
    data, not configuration, and shipping it under /etc made it a conffile:
    on any device where the file already existed -- they were hand-placed
    before the packages shipped them -- dpkg treated it as a local edit and
    preserved it, silently, for ever. Such a device never received a single
    packaged update to its descriptor. The old conffile is removed on
    upgrade; /etc/hifiberry/players.d still works and takes precedence, for
    an administrator who genuinely wants to override or add a player.
    Requires hifiberry-configurator 2.17.6, which reads both directories.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.9) stable; urgency=medium

  * postinst: do not restart config-server. When the web interface installs
    an extension, config-server is the process running apt and it holds the
    install job the UI polls in memory, so restarting from the postinst --
    which runs during dpkg's configure step, mid-install -- destroyed that
    job. The package installed correctly (apt runs under systemd-run and
    survives) but the UI reported the install as lost. The restart was also
    unnecessary: configurator re-reads conf.d in place after every extension
    install.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 18:30:00 +0000

hifiberry-librespot (0.8.0.8) stable; urgency=medium

  * Publish as a HiFiBerry extension: declare the XB-Extension metadata so
    the package appears in the web interface's extension catalog, and drop
    it from the hbos-full bundle. Spotify Connect is now installed on
    demand rather than shipped with every full image.
  * Ship the WebUI player descriptor and its icon
    (/etc/hifiberry/players.d/librespot.json). These were never packaged --
    dpkg -S found no owner for them on a running device -- so Spotify
    appeared in the Players page only where someone had placed the files by
    hand. As an extension that gap would be fatal: installing it would
    register no player at all.
  * The descriptor declares conflicts_with: ["soloist"], so the pairing is
    now stated from both sides rather than relying on Soloist alone to
    declare it.

